so recently i got a really bad cold and  spent a couple of days on the couch  watching daytime tv and old movies like  you have done  i grabbed a couple of cough drops to  suit the pain of a sore throat  long story short i wondered why cough  drops made my throat feel better  and well here we are with the video  today cough drops are just hard candy  with an extra ingredient  menthol menthol is a compound  traditionally extracted from the  peppermint plant  or more recently synthesized from either  mycerine or m-cresol  cough drops get their medicinal  properties through menthol which causes  pain relief by binding to two different  receptors in your peripheral nervous  system  in your throat one of these receptors  trpm8  causes a cool sensation associated with  menthol when menthol binds to the trpm8  receptor  it causes calcium to flow into the  calcium channels  signaling the brain to interpret a cool  sensation when you put a cough drop in  your mouth or rub a menthol-based cream  on your skin you feel a cooling  sensation  due to interaction with the trpm8  receptor  but what about the pain relief from a  sore throat well that has to do with the  kappa opioid receptor  the kappa opioid receptor is found in  the brain spinal cord and like the trpm8  receptor  and sensory neurons throughout your  entire body including in your throat  kappa opioid receptors have several  different functions  such as modulating pain consciousness  and mood with the most notable being  pain modulation when certain molecules  bind to the kappa receptor  it releases dopamine producing analgesic  effects  one notorious group of molecules that  causes a large release of dopamine when  taken  are opiates opiates such as heroin  morphine  oxycodone and others bind to the kappa  opioid receptors  inducing pain relief and euphoric  effects among the vast list of molecules  that bind to the kappa receptor  is menthol when menthol binds to kappa  receptors in your throat it induces the  analgesic effect  thereby relieving the pain for a short  period of time however  you may be asking why doesn't menthol  cause a strong pain relief effect like  heroin does  if it also binds to the same receptor  this is because menthol is a weak  agonist  an agonist is a molecule that when it  binds to a receptor  triggers that receptor's biological  response a strong agonist  binds firmly to the receptor and  therefore induces a stronger response  heroin is a very strong agonist and  binds very tightly to the kappa receptor  menthol on the other hand is a very weak  agonist and only produces  a small response this is why you have to  keep taking cough drops to feel the  effects of pain relief  and why a single dose of heroin makes  you feel euphoric  but wait there's more not all cough  drops are created equally  yeah i bet you thought they were all the  same yeah sure  companies make different cough drops for  different reasons  i'm going to use halls as an example  because i use hauls when i'm typically  sick  so i know the actual ingredients and no  this is not a paid product endorsement  so with hulls there's the traditional  kind of cough drop like the kind i  mentioned earlier in the video where the  active ingredient is menthol  specifically hall's relief cough drops  have 7.5 milligrams of menthol per cough  drop  and reduces coughing and throat pain  hence the relief label  hall soothing cough drops have less  menthol 2.5 milligrams to be exact  and act as a watered-down cough drop to  have a less harsh menthol sensation  hall's defense cough drops don't have  any menthol at all but rather 135  milligrams of vitamin c  to boost your immune system and act as a  cold preventative  finally hall's breezers also contains no  menthol but  instead has seven milligrams of pectin a  polysaccharide that coats the mucous  membranes in your throat to reduce  coughing  soreness and make breathing easier  you
 

